  "problem": "A certain company produces two types of footwear: casual and sports. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of footwear each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Casual** | **Sports** |\n| To: | **Casual** | $0.55$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Sports** | $0.45$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $45%$ of casual customers switch to sports each year, and $70%$ of sports customers change to casual.\n\nCurrently, $700\\,000$ customers purchase casual footwear, while $300\\,000$ customers purchase sports footwear. How many customers will purchase sports footwear in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change.",
